# Rules

Follow these rules when changing this repository.

## Process Rules

Before making a plan or any changes:

- Read `README.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, and the full contents of `docs/`.

Before presenting any changes to the user:

- Run `ruff check .`, `python tools/validate_skills.py`, and `python -m pytest`; all must pass.
- Run `python tools/run_snippets.py --demo` for every skill whose code you changed.
- Never run skill code that drives voltage on a real device unless the user has confirmed the wiring.
- Update `docs/` and `README.md` to match your changes, including `docs/verification.md`.
- Follow the branching process in `CONTRIBUTING.md`.
- Never bump version numbers unless asked.

## Skill Rules

- Keep each skill atomic, concise, and under 140 lines.
- Include only critical information, exact commands, and exact code. No background explanations.
- Link to Digilent's documentation instead of restating it.
- Use only SDK functions, constants, and samples that exist; the validator checks them.
- Never include code that has not been run.
- Frontmatter `name` equals the folder name; `description` says what the skill does and when to use it.

## Python Rules

- Use an object-oriented style: no one-off functions except `main`-style entry points; no module-level variables.
- Type all function parameters and return values. Use built-in generics and `X | None`.
- Use `pathlib` instead of `os.path`.
- Put all imports at the top of the file, grouped stdlib, third-party, then local.
- Give every file, class, and function a short docstring describing its purpose, with `Args`, `Returns`, and `Raises` sections where applicable.
- Keep lines at 100 characters or fewer.
- Never leave unused code or lint errors.
